><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="4" rect="609.449000,614.150000,793.449000,706.150000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250502154756+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250502154802+10'00'" name="ccd33d34-9708-4345-b2a8-3b71563256f4" page="9" coords="380.114000,161.453000,421.791000,161.453000,380.114000,150.727000,421.791000,150.727000" rect="379.779000,150.392000,422.126000,161.788000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>When investigating peak force and IMU accelerations during&#xD;gymnastics-style impact tasks (onto 100 mm gymnastics&#xD;mats), data can either be filtered (fourth-order Butterworth&#xD;filter with a minimum cut-off frequency of 85 Hz) or kept as&#xD;the raw signal. Results indicate that by applying the optimal&#xD;filter, the exported results showed small reductions in mean&#xD;peak variables which do not change the outcome of the&#xD;calculated means, although few individuals with large peaks&#xD;were subject to some over-smoothing effects. Using raw or&#xD;filtered data is suitable for these types of landing impact&#xD;skills, however the primary purpose of the data and how&#xD;the results will be interpreted should be considered prior to&#xD;finalising the data analysis procedure. The authors suggest&#xD;that for research purposes where a large number of participants’&#xD;data are pooled, acceleration and force data should&#xD;be filtered with a minimum cut-off frequency of 85 Hz to&#xD;ensure that excess noise is removed from the signals. While&#xD;for applied purposes, such as daily monitoring and injury&#xD;risk screening where results are analysed for an individual,&#xD;filtering should not be used as the raw signals are still&#xD;accurate, greater peaks are maintained, and the data analysis&#xD;procedure is simplified.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="9" rect="609.449000,69.452800,793.449000,161.453000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250502154806+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250502154906+10'00'" name="6a4eec96-1d44-49c7-8ffa-e39eccf97908" page="9" coords="310.734000,432.518000,362.583000,432.518000,310.734000,420.474000,362.583000,420.474000" rect="310.357000,420.098000,362.960000,432.894000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14a: both the research aims were answered.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
